Z||000003||Screwie||10-25-2001||12:44 PM||||Ken: Well, you'd still be able to trace through the time dimension if you lost em  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyway, if Canadians have to do that in the US, what the heck do Europeans have to do if they lose their job?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>isthiswh...etc: So it's pretty much the same as for Europeans going to the US and/or Canada. I thought there was some kind of agreement or something between Canada and the US. I hate that visa/green card stuff, I wish we could just go wherever we wanted  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||thomas_l||10-25-2001||01:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Screwie:<BR><B>Anyway, if Canadians have to do that in the US, what the heck do Europeans have to do if they lose their job?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Watch out for the green bus (La Migre)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>In North America, work permits are required in different countries but (at least until a month ago) travel between Canada, US, Mexico, and some of the Caribbean island nations is fairly unrestricted. Only proof of citizenship is required (i.e. a certified birth certificate). US drivers licenses, registration, and most insurance works in Canada too. Mexico is kinda hinkey because their gov't requires Mexican insurance. But then, I don't really want to take my car to Mexico anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Canada trips are way cool because a $100 withdrawl from an ATM shows on my bank statement as $65  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
